# International Organizations: Who Sits Where, Founded When

> The United Nations family, the Bretton Woods twins, the regional
> clubs — international organizations are the plumbing of world
> politics, and general-knowledge papers test the plumbing: founding
> years, headquarters cities, and who belongs. The trick, as always,
> is that the questions target the pairs people swap — the IMF is not
> the World Bank, and SAARC is not ASEAN.

## 1. The United Nations and Its Specialized Family

[definition]
The **United Nations** was founded on 24 October 1945 by 51 founding
members (India among them, though still a British colony — a
favourite fact). It has **six principal organs**: the General
Assembly, the Security Council, the Economic and Social Council
(ECOSOC), the Trusteeship Council, the International Court of
Justice, and the Secretariat.
[/definition]

| Body | Headquarters | Founded | Note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| UN | New York | 1945 | 193 members; 24 October = UN Day |
| Security Council | New York | 1945 | 15 members; 5 permanent with veto |
| ICJ (World Court) | The Hague, Netherlands | 1945 | UN's principal judicial organ |
| WHO | Geneva | 1948 | World Health Day: 7 April |
| UNESCO | Paris | 1945/1946 | Education, science, culture |
| UNICEF | New York | 1946 | Children's fund |
| ILO | Geneva | 1919 (oldest agency, League-era) | Labour standards |
| FAO | Rome | 1945 | Food and Agriculture |
| IMF | Washington, D.C. | 1944 (Bretton Woods) | Monetary stability |
| World Bank (IBRD) | Washington, D.C. | 1944 (Bretton Woods) | Reconstruction and development |
| WTO | Geneva | 1995 | Successor to GATT (1947) |
| IAEA | Vienna | 1957 | Atomic energy ("Atoms for Peace") |
| UNHCR | Geneva | 1950 | Refugees |
| UNDP | New York | 1965 | Development |
| ITC / WIPO | Geneva | — | Trade and intellectual property |

The Hague is the answer papers want for the **ICJ** — but the
International Criminal Court (ICC), a separate body not part of the
UN, also sits at The Hague. The trap question "Where is the
headquarters of the ICC?" expects the same city, and the deeper trap
asks which court is the UN's (the ICJ, not the ICC).

## 2. The Bretton Woods Twins and the Trade System

The **IMF and the World Bank** were both conceived at the Bretton
Woods Conference (July 1944, New Hampshire, USA) — 44 nations
including India. The IMF watches over exchange-rate stability and
lends to countries in balance-of-payments crisis; the World Bank
lends for development projects. Their **Special Drawing Right (SDR)**
is the IMF's unit of account, a fact that migrates into economics
papers.

The **WTO** began on 1 January 1995 as the successor to the **GATT**
(1947), moving from a provisional trade agreement to a full
organization with a dispute-settlement mechanism. Its headquarters —
unlike the Bretton Woods twins — is **Geneva**, a favourite swap
question. The WTO's ministerial conference is its top decision
making body; India hosted no ministerial until the discipline of
"who hosted MC-13" entered current-affairs territory.

## 3. The Regional and Strategic Clubs

| Organization | HQ / Secretariat | Founded | Members / note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| SAARC | Kathmandu | 1985 | 8 South Asian members; India a founder |
| ASEAN | Jakarta | 1967 | 10 South-East Asian members |
| European Union (EU) | Brussels | 1993 (Maastricht Treaty) | Eurozone subset uses euro |
| NATO | Brussels | 1949 | 32 members after 2023–24 enlargements |
| OPEC | Vienna | 1960 | Petroleum exporters; India not a member |
| GCC | Riyadh | 1981 | Six Gulf monarchies |
| BRICS | (Shanghai: NDB HQ) | 2009 first summit (2006 concept) | Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa |
| SCO (Shanghai Cooperation Organisation) | Beijing | 2001 | India joined 2017 |
| G20 | No permanent HQ (rotating presidency) | 1999 | India hosted the 2023 summit |
| African Union | Addis Ababa | 2002 (successor to OAU 1963) | 55 members |
| OAS | Washington, D.C. | 1948 | Americas |
| BIMSTEC | Dhaka (sec.) | 1997 | Bay of Bengal rim; India a member |
| Quad | No secretariat | 2007 (revived 2017) | India, USA, Japan, Australia |

The highest-frequency confusion is **SAARC vs ASEAN**: SAARC
(Kathmandu, 1985, eight South Asian members) versus ASEAN
(Jakarta, 1967, ten South-East Asian members). A paper that asks
"which of these is not a SAARC member" will offer an ASEAN country —
usually Myanmar (which interestingly is an ASEAN member but attended
SAARC as an observer).

India's memberships to keep straight: founding member of the UN
(1945) and SAARC (1985); member of BRICS, SCO (2017), G20, BIMSTEC
and the Quad; **not** a member of ASEAN, NATO, OPEC, the EU, or the
African Union. "Not a member" questions test exactly this boundary.

## 4. Humanitarian, Legal, and Sporting Bodies

The **International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement** was born
from Henry Dunant's experience at Solferino (1859); its
international committee (ICRC) sits in **Geneva**, and Dunant
became the first Nobel Peace Prize laureate (1901). **Interpol** —
police cooperation, not an arresting force — is headquartered in
**Lyon, France**, a city that surprises candidates expecting a
Geneva answer.

**Amnesty International** is based in **London**, and the Worldwide
Fund for Nature (WWF) in **Gland, Switzerland**. For sport, the IOC
sits in **Lausanne, Switzerland**, FIFA in **Zurich**, and the ICC
(cricket) in **Dubai** — a trio that regularly closes an
organizations round. The Mumbai-centered trap option for cricket's
ICC belongs to the BCCI, its Indian member body, not the
international federation.

[example]
A combined question: "Match the organization with its headquarters —
(a) IAEA, (b) Interpol, (c) ICJ, (d) ICC (cricket)." The answers:
Vienna, Lyon, The Hague, Dubai. Every one of the four has a famous
decoy — Geneva for IAEA, The Hague (again) for Interpol, New York
for ICJ, and Mumbai (BCCI) for the cricket ICC. Organizations
questions are usually headquarters questions wearing a costume.
[/example]

[summary]
Core dates: UN 1945 (New York, six organs, India a founder), WHO
1948 (Geneva), IMF and World Bank 1944 Bretton Woods (Washington
D.C.), WTO 1995 (Geneva, after GATT 1947), SAARC 1985 (Kathmandu),
ASEAN 1967 (Jakarta), NATO 1949 (Brussels), EU 1993 (Brussels),
OPEC 1960 (Vienna), SCO 2001 (Beijing). Courts: ICJ and ICC both at
The Hague, but only the ICJ belongs to the UN. The finisher set:
Red Cross Geneva, Interpol Lyon, Amnesty London, IOC Lausanne, FIFA
Zurich, cricket's ICC Dubai. India: UN founder, SAARC founder,
BRICS/SCO/G20/Quad member — but never ASEAN or NATO.
[/summary]
